<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Domain of Matthew Csák</h1><p class="st-meta">1297 CE – 1321 CE (approx.) · capital Trencsén</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Vast private domain of the magnate Matthew Csák in north-western Hungary, ruled like an independent state during the struggle for the crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-iii-csak">Matthew III Csák</a>, of a leading baronial clan, built up power in the Váh valley in the 1290s as royal authority collapsed. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-iii-of-hungary">Andrew III</a> in 1301 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> line, he backed rival claimants and seized castles across north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trencsén</a> (Trenčín).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>At its height he controlled some fourteen counties in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, with around 50 castles. He kept his own court, palatine and household officials, granted lands, made treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a> and ran justice and taxation like a king. He refused to submit to Charles I, and the pope excommunicated him in 1311.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Charles I defeated his allies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rozgony">Rozgony</a> in 1312 but could not take his heartland. After Matthew died in 1321, royal armies quickly took his castles, and the domain returned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary</a> (1301-1526).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1297 CE – 1321 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.51%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1297 CE – 1321 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Trencsén</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Matthew Csák</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian; Slovak</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lands of Matthew Csák, Csák's province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1309&amp;focus=domain-of-matthew-csak" aria-label="Domain of Matthew Csák on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5121 475 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Domain of Matthew Csák lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5441 731h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5441 731h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Slovakia. <a href="/atlas?year=1309&amp;focus=domain-of-matthew-csak">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
